  "status": "published",
  "title": "America Removed Maduro. Now It's Strengthening Venezuela's Military.",
  "deck": "Less than a year after U.S. forces captured Venezuela's president, Washington is establishing a military partnership with the government he left behind.",
  "category": "world",
  "categoryLabel": "World",
  "type": "Analysis",
  "author": "EP News",
  "publishedAt": "2026-11-19T10:40:00Z",
  "updatedAt": "2026-11-19T10:40:00Z",
  "body": [
    "In January, American special forces entered Venezuela and captured President Nicolás Maduro. Ten months later, the United States is preparing to train and strengthen the armed forces that served under his government.",
    "U.S. Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth announced a new military cooperation agreement Wednesday during a visit to Caracas, marking a dramatic reversal in relations between Washington and Venezuela.",
    "The agreement includes joint military training, intelligence sharing and expanded security cooperation. Potential improvements to Venezuelan military equipment and capabilities are also part of the broader partnership.",
    "Standing alongside acting Venezuelan President Delcy Rodríguez, Maduro's former vice president, Hegseth presented the agreement as the beginning of a new security relationship.",
    "The development raises a larger geopolitical question: Was removing Maduro primarily about changing Venezuela's government, or was it about changing Venezuela's relationship with the United States?",
    "<h2>From adversary to security partner</h2>",
    "For years, Washington regarded Venezuela's government as a regional security threat.",
    "Maduro's administration maintained close relationships with Russia, China and Iran while facing American sanctions and accusations of involvement in international drug trafficking.",
    "But the removal of Maduro did not dismantle Venezuela's entire governing structure.",
    "Rodríguez emerged as the country's acting president, and Washington has increasingly worked with her administration.",
    "The latest military agreement takes that relationship further by establishing direct cooperation with Venezuelan defense institutions.",
    "The Trump administration argues that cooperation can improve regional security, combat criminal organizations and stabilize Venezuela.",
    "The reversal illustrates how quickly Washington's relationship with a foreign government can change when its strategic alignment shifts.",
    "<h2>Oil and military power</h2>",
    "The defense agreement is unfolding alongside a significant expansion of American involvement in Venezuela's energy industry.",
    "Reuters reported that a separate U.S.-backed oil arrangement provides long-term access to 17 Venezuelan oilfields.",
    "Hegseth also connected Venezuelan energy production to the Western Hemisphere's vulnerability to disruptions in the Strait of Hormuz, a critical shipping route affected by the conflict with Iran.",
    "The connection is strategically important.",
    "Venezuela possesses enormous oil reserves, occupies a strategically valuable position along the Caribbean and has historically maintained relationships with America's geopolitical competitors.",
    "Closer cooperation with Caracas could give Washington greater influence over regional security and energy supplies while reducing opportunities for rival powers to expand their presence.",
    "However, Venezuela's deteriorated oil infrastructure remains a significant obstacle to rapidly increasing production. Greater access to Venezuelan oil does not automatically eliminate American exposure to global energy disruptions.",
    "<h2>What happened to democratic reform?</h2>",
    "While military and economic cooperation are advancing, Venezuela's political future remains unresolved.",
    "Opposition leader María Corina Machado returned to Venezuela in October, but no date has been announced for the country's next presidential election.",
    "That creates a difficult question for Washington.",
    "Can the United States pursue democratic reform while simultaneously strengthening military institutions that operated under the previous government?",
    "Supporters of the partnership could argue that cooperation offers Washington greater leverage to professionalize Venezuela's military and encourage institutional reform.",
    "Critics could question whether American security and energy priorities are advancing faster than commitments to political change.",
    "The agreement's full terms have not been publicly disclosed, leaving uncertainty about what conditions, if any, Washington has placed on its military assistance.",
    "<h2>The bigger geopolitical picture</h2>",
    "The United States has increasingly emphasized the Western Hemisphere as a national security priority.",
    "Venezuela's transition from adversary to partner could become one of the clearest examples of that strategy.",
    "Rather than attempting to rebuild Venezuela's political system from the ground up, Washington is working with existing institutions while seeking to redirect the country's strategic relationships.",
    "Whether that approach ultimately produces democratic reform, greater regional stability or simply a government more closely aligned with American interests remains uncertain.",
    "But one development is already clear.",
    "Less than a year after American forces removed Venezuela's president, Washington is now helping strengthen the military institutions that survived him.",
    "The government changed at the top. The strategic relationship is changing far more dramatically.",
    "EP News — Beyond the Headlines."
  ],
